exports[`../../../examples/crm/smithable.yml > file list and generated code > README.md 1`] = `
"# Acme CRM

Lightweight CRM for a small B2B sales team.

A [TanStack Start](https://tanstack.com/start) app with [Drizzle](https://orm.drizzle.team) and SQLite, generated by Smithable from \`smithable.yml\`.

## Run it

\`\`\`sh
pnpm install
pnpm db:seed    # optional: sample data
pnpm dev        # http://localhost:3000
pnpm test       # tests
\`\`\`

Build and run a container (data is kept in the \`/data\` volume):

\`\`\`sh
docker build -t acme-crm .
docker run -p 3000:3000 -v acme-crm-data:/data acme-crm
\`\`\`


## Signing in

Acme CRM signs users in with Google, through [Better Auth](https://www.better-auth.com). In development, the sign-in page also lets you sign in as any role (admin, sales) without a provider; production builds leave that out.

In production, set \`BETTER_AUTH_SECRET\` (at least 32 random characters, e.g. \`openssl rand -base64 32\`) and \`BETTER_AUTH_URL\` (the app's public address) as well.

### Google

1. In the [Google Cloud console](https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials), create an OAuth client ID of type *Web application*.
2. Add the redirect URLs \`http://localhost:3000/api/auth/callback/google\` and \`https://<your domain>/api/auth/callback/google\`.
3. Put the keys in \`.env\` (never commit it):

   \`\`\`sh
   GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=...
   GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=...
   \`\`\`

## Users and invitations

The team page lists who has access and lets administrators invite people by email address with a role, change roles, and remove users. An invitation mail carries a link to the sign-in page; the invited address then signs in with a configured provider and has the invited role. The link is also shown on the page, for sending by hand when the app has no mail settings (see Mail). Invitations are valid for 14 days and can be resent or withdrawn.

Sign-up is closed (see \`selfSignUp\` under \`signIn\` in \`smithable.auth.yml\`): only invited addresses and the \`admins\` of \`smithable.auth.yml\` can sign in. Everyone else is refused at sign-in and told to ask an administrator.
## Mail

Hooks (\`src/custom/hooks.ts\`) and custom code send mail with \`sendMail\` from \`src/kit/mail.ts\`. It reads SMTP settings from the environment: \`SMTP_URL\` (\`smtp://user:password@host:587\`, or \`smtps://\` for TLS from the start) or \`SMTP_HOST\`, \`SMTP_PORT\`, \`SMTP_USER\` and \`SMTP_PASSWORD\`, plus \`MAIL_FROM\` (the sender). Any provider works; give its SPF, DKIM and DMARC records to the sender's domain so the mail arrives. Without settings nothing is sent: mails are written to \`data/mail/outbox\` as \`.eml\` files, which is what development and tests look at.

## Limits

A signed-in person may make 60 changes a minute, a visitor 10 a minute per address, and an administrator may send 20 invitations an hour; beyond that a change is refused with "Too many changes in a short time" until the minute is over. The numbers are in \`src/kit/limit.ts\` (\`LIMITS\`), counted in memory by the running process; sign-in attempts are limited by Better Auth itself (3 per 10 seconds). Behind a proxy that sets \`X-Forwarded-For\`, the address is the one the proxy saw.

## Custom styles

Your own CSS goes in \`src/custom/styles.css\`, which is never overwritten and wins over the kit's styles. Rely on these attributes, not on class names or the order of elements; they name what a part is, and a value is removed only in a major version:

| Attribute | On | Values |
|---|---|---|
| \`data-page\` | \`<body>\` | the page's name in the spec, e.g. \`customers\`, \`home\` |
| \`data-kit\` | a kit component's root | \`app-shell\`, \`nav\`, \`model-table\`, \`model-form\`, \`field\`, \`search-box\`, \`dashboard\`, \`tile\`, \`calendar\`, \`reserve\`, \`landing\`, \`section\`, \`users\`, \`sign-in\`, \`onboarding\` |
| \`data-part\` | inside a component | \`header\`, \`title\`, \`actions\`, \`row\`, \`cell\`, \`label\`, \`input\`, \`help\`, \`error\`, \`empty\`, \`footer\` |
| \`data-field\` | a field's wrapper, input and table cells | the field's name |
| \`data-section\` | a landing section | its name, e.g. \`hero\` |
| \`data-tone\` | a badge | \`neutral\`, \`success\`, \`warning\`, \`danger\` |

For example \`[data-kit="model-form"] [data-part="actions"] { justify-content: flex-start; }\`. The theme's colours are CSS variables (\`var(--primary)\`, see \`src/theme.css\`), and Tailwind's \`@apply\` works in the file too.

## Licences

The app's code is yours: the app kit (\`src/kit/\`) comes under the Zero-Clause BSD licence, which asks nothing of you, and the generated code carries no licence of its own. The components in \`src/components/ui/\` are shadcn/ui's (MIT); their notice is in \`LICENSES/shadcn-ui.txt\` and travels with the container image. Libraries from npm keep their own licences in \`node_modules/\`.


## Project structure

| Path | Owner | What it is |
|---|---|---|
| \`smithable.yml\` | You | What the app does. Smithable regenerates the code from it |
| \`src/generated/\`, \`src/db/schema.ts\`, \`src/db/seed.ts\`, generated routes | Smithable | Regenerated on every change. Files start with a "Generated by Smithable" comment |
| \`src/kit/\`, \`src/components/ui/\` | Smithable | Generic, readable building blocks, updated with Smithable. Copy one into \`src/custom/\` to change it |
| \`src/custom/\` | You | Your own code: actions, queries, custom pages, and \`hooks.ts\` (what happens after records change, and once a day). Smithable never overwrites it |
| \`AGENTS.md\` | Smithable | How AI coding agents should work on the app: through Smithable's MCP tools |
| \`drizzle/\` | History | Database migrations. Never rewritten once created |

The app has no dependency on Smithable: it builds and runs with the ordinary tools above.
"
`;

